WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.700
So one of the fossils that they began to find was this

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.380
a trilobite and that's actually

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:07.721
what Otto was drawing over here.

00:00:07.721 --> 00:00:10.740
The trilobite began to emerge in the Cambrian

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.660
and the Silurian so they're very very old.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:17.100
And Americans began to find these in the railroad cuts

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.779
that they were making through the rocks

00:00:18.779 --> 00:00:21.500
of the United States. They began to

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.029
talk to people in England who were also

00:00:24.029 --> 00:00:27.580
finding trilobites. And they began for the first time, now

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:30.140
again we take this for granted, but it was

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:33.340
not until the 1830s that people in Britain

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.980
and the Americas began to imagine

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:39.500
that those two areas of land had once been connected.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:44.520
So this is a new geological understanding of

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.940
the past that is beginning to stretch
very far back in time.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:53.420
So next time you see a trilobite be excited about it.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.500
The Industrial Revolution is also important

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.740
because they are finding evidence of a time

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:04.100
in the past in Earth's history that was full

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:06.100
of what they now called and

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:10.380
this is a new term coined in the 1830s "fossil fuels."

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:12.940
Fossil fuels is a term that is coined

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.260
in the early 19th century precisely

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:17.220
during the deep time revolution.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:18.840
They're cutting through the earth.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:20.300
They're looking for fuel

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:22.160
to power the Industrial Revolution.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.680
They find an era, a lost era, full of palm trees

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:29.020
from a time when the whole Earth

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:31.160
was like the Amazon. A kind of

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:35.020
what they called a "Carboniferous," an era of carbon.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:36.880
And this was going to become,

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.120
here's another key word from this time period:

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.360
the "natural resource" that

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:43.380
would power the Industrial Revolution.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.260
So the Industrial Revolution,
the cars, the trains,

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:49.520
are all part of the new ways of approaching the Earth

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.840
that helped get us to deep time.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:56.640
Deep time is great, but it brings up

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.520
a bunch of new problems. And these are so interesting.

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:04.040
And I give them to you, not as me telling you

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:07.620
what to think, but as partners with me in thinking

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:10.060
about how difficult this is to think about.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:12.660
So deep time presents a number of issues.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:13.890
Let me just give you the first one.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:15.760
The first thing is that deep time

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.900
is an inconceivable concept. Charles Darwin

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.180
is one of the many people in the 19th century

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:22.710
who thinks about deep time right

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:25.080
evolution has to happen over deep time.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:29.600
And he says it is incomprehensively vast.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:33.300
He is one of many others who look at the idea

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:36.460
of deep time, that the Earth is not 6,000

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:38.240
but maybe 10 million... 20 million...

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:43.760
4 billion years old... And says that may be so

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.940
but I cannot conceive of that.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:50.820
So it's this startling new concept that

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:54.780
by its very nature is an inconceivable concept.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:58.020
So this is  wonderful for historians like me

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:00.840
who like to imagine how people think about their world

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:04.600
to imagine a new idea that you cannot think about

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:06.980
is an absolutely extraordinary thing

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.360
that is very very fun for us to think about.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:14.140
Deep Time also has a second problem with it

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:19.220
which is that it is existentially horrifying.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:22.120
One thing we know about human beings

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:25.360
is that we like to be the center of the universe.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:28.820
Nothing matters unless we are part of it.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:33.040
Unless we are folded into our 
own history of the universe.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:34.540
And the history of the Earth

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:39.240
it's hard to understand why it should matter.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:43.040
And deep time presents this problem

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.200
in a really palpable way.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.440
The Bible ultimately is about human beings

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:53.260
and how you get from then to now.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:56.300
And the Bible has at its very center

00:03:56.300 --> 00:04:00.380
the sense that human beings are God's special creation.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:04.200
Deep time does not have that assurance.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:08.300
It removes humanity from the story of life

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:11.620
on Earth. From the story of Earth.

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:13.520
From the story of the cosmos.

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:17.300
You can have as many strata as you want

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:20.720
But if none of them contain human beings

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:23.580
and if none of them contain the things

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.500
that human beings are good at doing

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:28.600
which is having events. We have events.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:30.540
We have events like this one and

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:34.520
events leave documents. They leave
traces in the historical record.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:38.780
None of those are present in these strata.

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:42.940
So the strata present to people

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:45.080
who could not before have imagined

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:47.660
anything longer than a 6,000 year history

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:49.920
that was in a document

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:51.520
it was in the Bible.

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:54.400
The Bible had nothing to say about strata.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.940
So this was very very unsettling.

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:01.740
A book in which we were not...

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:08.560
not just the main actors, but not actors at all.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:12.720
So what do you do to fix this problem?

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:15.520
Well in the nineteenth century we see

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:19.000
a number of solutions emerging to this problem.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:23.000
Which is to try to make the inconceivable concept

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:27.500
of deep time conceivable. Not only conceivable,

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:30.680
but as we saw with the slinky unwinding

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:33.200
of the Earth's history (in the thing

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.699
that you're gonna look at later on when you go home)

00:05:35.699 --> 00:05:39.240
to make it beautiful, to make it relatable.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:43.700
To make it in some ways part of our own humanity

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:46.040
When in fact we know deep down

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:49.400
inside that deep time

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:53.760
doesn't really have humanity necessarily as part of it.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:56.600
So what do some of these images look like?

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:58.640
Some of these are going to be familiar to you.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:02.080
But I hope that from now on you look at them as

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:05.200
the evidence of the birth of deep time.

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:08.600
So this is one of the many images to come out of some

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:11.560
of the Grand Canyon exploring expeditions

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:15.740
of the 1870s 1880s and 1890s.

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:19.500
This is William Henry Holmes' 
painting of the Grand Canyon.

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:23.650
This is incredible, right? This is like strata central.

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:29.040
Looking down into the Grand Canyon you
see all of these layer cakes of time.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:32.420
and look at these teeny tiny human beings.

00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:36.360
They're totally insignificant in this larger canvas.

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:38.940
But it is so incredibly beautiful

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:41.080
that you don't care.

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:43.900
And it is so incredibly beautiful,

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:45.860
and I'm just giving you things to think about, right?

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:47.900
You don't have to agree with me just think about it.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:51.500
Bear with me. It is so incredibly beautiful

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:54.660
that even though it is existentially horrifying

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:58.860
even though we as human beings are insignificant

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:02.280
we imagine that perhaps something like the deity

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:06.360
might be there because it is so transcendently beautiful

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:09.280
and it makes us think about eternity

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:13.060
in the way that people in the 18th century

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:15.140
and before thought about eternity.

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:18.000
So one way to think about deep time images

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:22.960
is as the secularization of the Judeo-Christian idea

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.840
of the cosmic eternity in the face of

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:38.410
the scientific revolutions of the 18th and 19th century.

